; A tag wrapping a PRELUDE CONSTANT (`#6.11(true)`; equally `nil` / `false`, and the parenthesized
; `#6.11((true))`) is REJECTED GRACEFULLY, with the SAME message as its literal-inner sibling
; `#6.5(5)` (tests/robustness/tagged_literal.cddl): a rule whose whole body is a bare fixed value
; has no standalone type representation, so there is nothing for the tag to wrap.
;
; The two spellings reach that message from two different registration seams, which is why each
; carries its own guard over one shared message helper (`record_bare_fixed_rule_rejection`). A
; literal inner lands on the transparent-ALIAS seam (`register_type_alias`), which has always
; classified a bare `Fixed`. A prelude-constant inner resolves through the prelude alias
; (`true` -> Fixed(Bool)), and its tag head (or `@newtype`) forces the WRAPPER seam instead —
; `RustStruct::new_wrapper`, which would render the `Fixed` as the wrapper's inner member type.
; Guarding both seams is what makes the classification independent of which spelling the user
; picked. The tag itself is irrelevant to the gap: `#6.11([true])` wraps a GROUP, which does have a
; representation, and generates.
;
; Same underlying reason as the occurrence shape (tests/robustness/fixed_value_occurrence.cddl); the
; auto-wrapping tag-inner variants that ARE supported carry a `.default` or a range on the inner
; (tests/corpus/tagged_default_inner.cddl / tagged_ranged_inner.cddl) — those resolve to a primitive
; wrapper, not a bare `Fixed`.
t = #6.11(true)
